- op: discuss  # f6e2
  at: '2026-04-02T05:32:54Z'  # f6e2
  by: agent  # f6e2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f6e2
  clock: 11  # f6e2
  nonce: f6e2  # f6e2
  message: "Reviewing in light of the stale PR #33 discovery. The invariant statement is too narrow — it only covers 'branching from the wrong remote.' The actual failure cascade had three stages:\n\n1. **Wrong remote (covered by current fix):** Branched from origin/dev instead of selfh/dev. The pre-work playbook now checks CI_FAILOVER_ACTIVE. This part works.\n\n2. **Stale PRs not cleaned up (NOT covered):** When PRs #33 and #34 failed and I moved to a new branch for PR #37, I never closed #33. It sat open for 10+ hours until the human noticed. The playbook prevents creating wrong-base PRs going forward, but doesn't enforce cleanup of existing stale PRs before starting new work.\n\n3. **Ad-hoc API calls when disoriented (NOT covered):** When CI failures confused me, I abandoned the approved scripts (forgejo-api.sh) and wrote raw curl commands with wrong URL paths. This produced different errors that I misdiagnosed as token problems, compounding the confusion. The rule 'nothing ad-hoc, use approved scripts only' was already in AGENTS.md but I violated it under pressure.\n\nRegarding the human's git hook suggestion: a pre-push or pre-commit hook that prints the current failover state and authoritative remote would be a structural safeguard that doesn't depend on the agent remembering to check the playbook. Something like:\n\n  pre-push hook → if CI_FAILOVER_ACTIVE exists, print '⚠️ FAILOVER: authoritative remote is selfh, not origin' and verify the push target matches.\n\nThis would catch wrong-remote pushes at the git level, before they reach CI. It would also be a good place to warn about open stale PRs."  # f6e2
- op: update  # 45da
  at: '2026-04-02T05:33:47Z'  # 45da
  by: agent  # 45da
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 45da
  clock: 12  # 45da
  nonce: 45da  # 45da
  set:  # 45da
    fields:  # 45da
      statement: "During failover, all agent operations must be failover-aware: (1) branch/pull/rebase from the authoritative remote (selfh, not origin), (2) clean up stale PRs from failed attempts before starting new work, (3) use only approved scripts for API interactions — never ad-hoc curl/API calls. Violation of any of these causes compounding confusion that wastes hours."  # 45da
      root_cause: "Three gaps: (a) pre-work playbook hardcoded origin (fixed in PR #37), (b) no enforcement of stale PR cleanup when abandoning a failed PR for a new branch (PR #33 sat open 10+ hours), (c) no structural guard against bypassing approved scripts when disoriented — agent wrote raw curl with wrong URL paths, misdiagnosed as token issue."  # 45da
- op: discuss  # bdcd
  at: '2026-04-02T05:34:26Z'  # bdcd
  by: agent  # bdcd
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bdcd
  clock: 13  # bdcd
  nonce: bdcd  # bdcd
  message: "Responding to the git hook suggestion.\n\nYes, git hooks would be stronger than playbook instructions because they fire automatically — the agent can't forget to check them.\n\nSpecific hooks that would help:\n\n**1. pre-push hook (most impactful):**\n- If CI_FAILOVER_ACTIVE exists, verify the push target is selfh, not origin\n- If pushing to a PR ref (refs/for/dev/*), check for other open PRs from the same agent on the same topic (stale PR detection)\n- Print a clear state reminder: 'FAILOVER ACTIVE: authoritative remote is selfh'\n\n**2. post-checkout hook (for branch creation):**\n- When creating a new branch (git checkout -b), verify the current HEAD is reachable from the authoritative remote's dev\n- If HEAD is only reachable from the stale remote, warn loudly: 'WARNING: you branched from origin/dev which is N commits behind selfh/dev'\n\n**3. Claude Code session-start hook (already exists as startup hook):**\n- The startup hook already runs. It could include a failover state check that prints the authoritative remote and any open stale PRs.\n- This is where the 'state reminder' would live — it runs at the start of every conversation.\n\nThe pre-push hook is the most valuable because it's the last line of defense before a wrong-base PR hits CI. The post-checkout hook catches it earlier. The session-start hook catches it earliest.\n\nI'd recommend implementing the pre-push hook first since it prevents the most expensive failure (CI cycles wasted on divergent branches). It's also a governance file (.githooks/**) so it needs human approval."  # bdcd
- op: discuss  # 2d73
  at: '2026-04-02T05:43:38Z'  # 2d73
  by: human  # 2d73
  actor: jgstern  # 2d73
  clock: 14  # 2d73
  nonce: 2d73  # 2d73
  message: "pre-push hook sounds good, although I'm not sure how we would precisely check for stale PRs \"from the same agent on the same topic\"?"  # 2d73
- op: discuss  # 7b1d
  at: '2026-04-02T06:58:55Z'  # 7b1d
  by: agent  # 7b1d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7b1d
  clock: 15  # 7b1d
  nonce: 7b1d  # 7b1d
  message: "Replying to the stale PR detection question.\n\nYou're right — precisely matching 'same agent, same topic' is fragile. A simpler and more robust approach for the pre-push hook:\n\n**Option A: Skip stale PR detection in the hook entirely.** The pre-push hook focuses on what it can reliably check: (1) failover remote correctness, (2) base branch freshness. Stale PR cleanup is better handled by the session-start hook (which already runs at conversation start and can call `scripts/list-my-prs` to show all open PRs for awareness).\n\n**Option B: Warn about ALL open PRs from this user on push.** No topic matching needed — just list open PRs and let the agent/human decide if any are stale. Something like: 'You have 2 open PRs: #33 (cgo-linker-fix, 6h old), #37 (cgo-io-catalog). Is #33 still needed?' This is informational, not blocking.\n\nI'd recommend Option A (keep the hook simple) plus adding an open-PR listing to the session-start hook output. The pre-push hook would be:\n1. If CI_FAILOVER_ACTIVE exists, verify push target is selfh (block if origin)\n2. Verify the branch's merge-base with dev is fresh (warn if >10 commits behind)\n\nThis avoids the 'same topic' matching problem entirely. Want me to implement this?"  # 7b1d
